---
/*
 * The ruled field the whole site sits on.
 *
 * Every card, rule and annotation on this design is aligned to an implied grid.
 * This draws it. Two layers at different pitches move by different amounts as
 * the cursor crosses the page, which is what makes it read as depth rather than
 * as wallpaper — the coarse rule is nearer, so it travels further.
 *
 * Deliberately not a canvas. The whole thing is two repeating gradients and a
 * transform, which the compositor handles on its own; a canvas would mean a
 * render loop, a resize path and a device-pixel-ratio problem, to draw straight
 * lines that CSS already draws. The only script is the twenty lines that turn a
 * pointer position into two custom properties.
 *
 * It sits at z-index -1, which puts it above the body background — that
 * background propagates to the canvas and paints first — and below every piece
 * of ordinary content, without wrapping the page in anything.
 *
 * That last part matters more than it sounds. A wrapper with a z-index makes a
 * stacking context, and a stacking context traps `mix-blend-mode`: the cover
 * mark is composited against whatever is behind it, and inside a wrapper the
 * field is not behind it any more. The artwork's ground reappeared as a white
 * panel the moment a wrapper existed. No wrapper, no trap, and the grid reads
 * straight through the mark.
 *
 * Cards carry their own ground and occlude it, so the field shows through the
 * gutters and margins and stops at the edge of anything solid — which is the
 * correct reading: it is the paper, not a texture laid over the top.
 */
---

<div class="grid-field" data-grid-field aria-hidden="true"></div>

<style>
  .grid-field {
    position: fixed;
    /*
     * Overscanned well past the viewport so the parallax can never drag an edge
     * of the gradient into view at the extremes of travel.
     */
    inset: -8vmax;
    z-index: -1;
    pointer-events: none;

    /* Written by the pointer script; -1 to 1 on each axis, already eased. */
    --px: 0;
    --py: 0;
  }

  .grid-field::before,
  .grid-field::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background-image:
      repeating-linear-gradient(
        to right,
        var(--rule-ink) 0 1px,
        transparent 1px var(--cell)
      ),
      repeating-linear-gradient(
        to bottom,
        var(--rule-ink) 0 1px,
        transparent 1px var(--cell)
      );
    will-change: transform;
  }

  /* The construction grid: fine, far, barely there, and it moves least. */
  .grid-field::before {
    --cell: 34px;
    --rule-ink: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-rule) 42%, transparent);
    transform: translate3d(calc(var(--px) * 7px), calc(var(--py) * 7px), 0);
  }

  /* The datum grid: coarse, nearer, a shade stronger, and it travels further. */
  .grid-field::after {
    --cell: 170px;
    --rule-ink: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-rule) 78%, transparent);
    transform: translate3d(calc(var(--px) * 20px), calc(var(--py) * 20px), 0);
  }

  /*
   * Without a pointer there is no parallax to offer, and a permanently static
   * grid behind every line of body text is a texture nobody asked for. Coarse
   * layer only, and fainter.
   */
  @media (hover: none) {
    .grid-field::before {
      display: none;
    }
    .grid-field::after {
      --rule-ink: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-rule) 45%, transparent);
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .grid-field::before,
    .grid-field::after {
      transform: none;
      will-change: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
